 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Delivery uses self-hosted WebSockets: the delivery component owns socket sessions, and browsers reconnect and catch up after a drop. Committed changes reach connected browsers within the freshness target; subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store keeps replayable event revisions so a reconnecting browser can catch up from its last revision; delivery holds connection capacity and reliable publication; browsers run reconnect/catch-up logic rather than a polling loop or provider subscription. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its repeated reads and seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting trades those away for connection operations we own: capacity sizing, session cleanup, and reconnect behavior.
 
