 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** The delivery component holds socket sessions and publishes committed revisions; browsers reconnect and catch up from their last seen revision. The change store keeps replayable revisions to support catch-up. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for repeated reads and seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling. This trades deliberate connection and reconnect operations work for fast updates without vendor dependence.
